"""English source copy: explain decisions and interactions, not field lists."""
SIMULATION = "Defaults for every simulation. They take effect the next time you press Start and apply to all bodies that do not use their own simulation settings."
SHOW_COLLIDERS = "Collision shapes are the simplified geometry Newton uses for contact: a box, a sphere or a convex hull around each body. They appear as coloured outlines over the model and reveal where contact geometry differs from what you see. This option decides whether the display is on when a window opens; the menu command “Show collision shapes” switches it in the current window. The display never changes how bodies collide."
COLLIDER_SHORTCUT = "Opens IngeTrazo's shortcut settings at the command that switches the collision display. No shortcut is assigned by default."
GRAVITY = "Acceleration along the model's Z axis in m/s². −9.81 is Earth's gravity, 0 makes all bodies weightless and positive values pull upwards. Stronger gravity means more load for actuators."
GROUND = "Adds an invisible, infinite floor at height Z = 0 for bodies to land on. Switch it off when parts should move below Z = 0 or when the model has its own base."
WARN_GROUND = "At Start, the control panel lists movable bodies that reach below Z = 0 while ground contact is on: they scrape along the ground and distort the result. Bodies that only rest on Z = 0, fixed bodies and bodies without ground contact are not listed. The warning never prevents the start."
LINEAR_DAMPING = "Slows down moving bodies, similar to air resistance. At 1 /s a free body loses about two thirds of its speed per second; 0 switches damping off. More damping calms oscillations but also resists intended motion, so actuators need more force."
ANGULAR_DAMPING = "Like linear damping, but for rotation: slows down spinning and wobbling bodies. 0 switches it off."
QUALITY = "How exactly joints and contacts are solved. Quick suits setting up large mechanisms, Balanced everyday work, and Precise checking demanding contacts. Geometry and cable resolution stay the same, but motion can differ slightly between the levels."
HIDDEN_GEOMETRY = "Hidden groups and groups on hidden layers. When needed simulates a hidden body only if a visible body depends on it, for example as its reference body, attachment, connection or coupling, and keeps hidden static bodies as surroundings. All other hidden bodies are left out, so large models start faster. Always simulates every hidden body; Never leaves all of them out."
RUNTIME_SETUP = "Creates the runtime in your IngeTrazo data folder: a private Python 3.12 and MuJoCo 3.3.7. It starts only when you confirm, and removing it deletes only that folder. A manual alternative is described in docs/runtime-setup.md."
RUNTIME = "The Python interpreter of the environment that contains MuJoCo 3.3.7. Newton runs the simulation and the collision display in it as a separate process. Change this only when that environment has been moved or replaced; editing the model never requires it."
PROFILES = "A profile copies parameter values onto a body while preserving its placement and reference bodies. Bodies remember their profile, but keep local values until you explicitly sync them. Sync replaces profile parameters, including local edits; use the sidebar for one body or queue all linked bodies here for OK. Built-ins are read-only, but you can inspect their values and duplicate one as your own starting point."
BODY = "The collision shape decides where a body can move. A box or hull closes openings and can block a mechanism; child components keep the gaps open."
MOTION = "A servo holds a position, a motor holds a speed. Force limit, load and contacts decide what the body actually reaches."
LIMIT_HARDNESS = "How firmly the limits hold. At 100 % they act as hard stops and give way by only a few tenths of a degree, even in long chains of light links such as a cable drag chain. Lower values let them yield under load like a rubber buffer; 0 % is the simulator's own default softness, which in such chains can give way by tens of degrees."
CONNECTION = "A connection closes a mechanism, for example between connecting rod and slider, without changing the reference bodies. It must allow the motion of the existing axes; a fixed connection can lock the mechanism."
FLEXIBLE = "The line becomes a bendable strand of fixed length. A larger radius makes it stiffer and tighter in guides; stiff, finely segmented strands cost computation time."
COLLISIONS = "Exclude contact between parts that are already guided by a joint, such as a pin inside its bearing, when their overlap would otherwise jam the mechanism. The pair can then pass through each other, so the joint must provide the restraint. Contacts with other bodies remain active."
OVERRIDES = "Use overrides when this body needs a different environment. It still collides with and pulls on connected neighbours, so local gravity can move an entire mechanism. Switch overrides off to inherit the global defaults again; a zero gravity vector instead explicitly makes this body weightless."
FIELDS = "Combine attraction with a sideways starting velocity to create an orbit instead of a straight fall. Attraction grows near the centre; a larger softening radius keeps close passes from producing extreme forces. Initial velocity is applied only when starting a freely movable body."
COUPLING_FREE = "Couple axes when one control should move several parts, such as two gripper jaws. Limits on either side restrict the shared movement."
COUPLING_FOLLOWER = "This axis follows the leader's control. Direction and ratio set its relative movement; the leader supplies the force for both."
COUPLING_LEADER = "{bodies} follow this control. Their additional load and limits also affect the leading axis; select a follower to change how far or in which direction it moves."
PROFILE_OVERRIDES = "An enabled override replaces the global environment when this profile is applied. It can change the load on connected parts as well as the selected body; leave it off when the profile should follow the simulation's defaults."
